<tr><td>PetSmart.com</td><td>Pasadena, CA</td><td>PetSmart, Idealab (50/50 JV)<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-32">32</a></sup></td><td>$50M+ second round (Sep ’99)<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-33">33</a></sup></td><td>PetSmart catalog fulfillment<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-32">32</a></sup></td><td>Filed a $115M IPO<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-35">35</a></sup> and pulled it Nov 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-34">34</a></sup>. Absorbed by PetSmart. Bought the pets.com URL<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup></td></tr>
</table></div>
<div class="card rv" style="margin-top:26px">
<div class="card-head"><div><h3>Reported funding, by company</h3><p>$ millions. The bases aren’t comparable: the figures mix cumulative totals, single rounds and IPO proceeds.</p></div></div>
<div class="cbox"><canvas id="rivalChart"></canvas></div>
<div class="src">Pets.com private rounds and IPO<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup>. Petopia’s two 1999 rounds<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-28">28</a></sup>. Petstore.com’s round as reported by Variety<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-31">31</a></sup>. PetSmart.com’s second round (“more than $50M”) plus each parent’s $10M cash contribution<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-32">32</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-33">33</a></sup>. These are reported minimums.</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="timeline">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">09</span>Timeline</div><h2 class="rv">Seven years, 34 moments</h2><p class="lede rv">From incorporation to the final filing, with the market and the rivals alongside. Filter by thread.</p></div>
<div class="tl-filters" id="tlf"><button class="chip on" data-f="all">All</button><button class="chip" data-f="money">Money</button><button class="chip" data-f="marketing">Marketing</button><button class="chip" data-f="corporate">Corporate</button><button class="chip" data-f="death">Death & estate</button><button class="chip" data-f="market">Market</button><button class="chip" data-f="rivals">Rivals</button></div>
<div class="tlv" id="tlv"><div class="ev" data-cat="corporate"><div class="d"><b>Oct 7</b>1998</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Corporate</div><h4>Incorporated</h4><p>Pets.com, Inc. is incorporated in California.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="corporate"><div class="d"><b>Feb 17</b>1999</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Corporate</div><h4>Operations begin</h4><p>Buys the Pets.com domain and web store from a third party. The founding-era site described itself as “a division of WebMagic™”.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="corporate"><div class="d"><b>Mar 1999</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Corporate</div><h4>Julie Wainwright named CEO</h4><p>The former Reel.com chief executive joins as CEO and director. Chris Deyo follows as president in April.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="money"><div class="d"><b>Apr 1999</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Money</div><h4>$10.0M Series A and an Amazon ad deal</h4><p>5,781,862 Series A preferred shares. An advertising agreement with Amazon.com is dated Apr 22.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>May 14</b>1999</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>PetSmart and Idealab form PetSmart.com</h4><p>A 50/50 joint venture in Pasadena. Each parent contributes $10M in cash.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-32">32</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="money"><div class="d"><b>Jun 1999</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Money</div><h4>$50.0M Series B</h4><p>Amazon becomes the largest shareholder.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="corporate"><div class="d"><b>Jul 1</b>1999</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Corporate</div><h4>Union City distribution center</h4><p>Sublease of 143,232 sq ft at 33201 Dowe Ave. Web store version 2 launches in July.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Jul 1999</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>Petopia raises $66M</h4><p>The Petco-backed rival closes a large round.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-28">28</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Sep 27</b>1999</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>PetSmart.com raises $50M+</h4><p>A second round for the PetSmart/Idealab venture.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-33">33</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Nov 1999</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>Petstore.com raises $97M</h4><p>Discovery Communications becomes its largest shareholder.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-31">31</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="marketing"><div class="d"><b>Nov 25</b>1999</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Marketing</div><h4>Macy’s parade balloon</h4><p>A sock-puppet balloon flies in the Thanksgiving Day Parade.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="corporate"><div class="d"><b>Dec 1999</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Corporate</div><h4>Indiana DC leased; S-1 filed</h4><p>A distribution center in Greenwood, near Indianapolis, is leased Dec 7. Another $49.2M of preferred stock is raised in Nov–Dec, and the S-1 is filed.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Dec 1999</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>Petopia adds $35M</h4><p>Total funding is reported at about $114M.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-28">28</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="marketing"><div class="d"><b>Jan 18</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Marketing</div><h4>Disney/ABC airtime for stock</h4><p>1,378,000 Series C shares are issued for ABC network media time valued at $11.02M.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="marketing"><div class="d"><b>Jan 30</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Marketing</div><h4>Super Bowl XXXIV ad</h4><p>The sock puppet gets its own spot on ABC, widely reported at about $1.2M (secondary source).<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-38">38</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Feb 2000</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>PetSmart.com files a $115M IPO</h4><p>The stock market is still open to e-tailers.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-35">35</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="money"><div class="d"><b>Feb 11</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Money</div><h4>IPO at $11</h4><p>7.5M shares on Nasdaq as IPET: $82.5M gross, about $75.3M net.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="market"><div class="d"><b>Mar 10</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Market</div><h4>The Nasdaq peaks</h4><p>It closes at 5,048.62, its bubble high. By year-end it is 2,470.52.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-26">26</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="marketing"><div class="d"><b>Jun 13</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Marketing</div><h4>$20 sock puppets ship</h4><p>Slate calls it “The Puppet That Ate a Dot-Com.”<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-14">14</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="corporate"><div class="d"><b>Jul 2000</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Corporate</div><h4>Buys Petstore.com</h4><p>All stock: 5.8M common shares plus preferred stock. It brings in $3M of cash.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-7">7</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="money"><div class="d"><b>Sep 30</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Money</div><h4>$23.1M cash left</h4><p>Cumulative net loss: $146.6M. Merrill Lynch is shopping the company.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-4">4</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Oct 2000</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>Petopia lays off most staff</h4><p>About 60% of employees are cut.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-30">30</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Nov 7</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Wind-down announced</h4><p>About 255 of 320 staff are laid off and IPET closes at $0.22. “No party was prepared to provide capital or acquire the company.”<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Nov 9</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>The store closes</h4><p>“We have closed our virtual doors.” Orders placed before 11am PT ship.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Nov 30</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>PetSmart.com pulls its IPO</h4><p>PetSmart later raises its stake to 81%.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-34">34</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="rivals"><div class="d"><b>Dec 2000</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Rivals</div><h4>Petco buys Petopia’s assets</h4><p>Petopia cancels its planned IPO in Feb 2001.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-30">30</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-29">29</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Dec 2000</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Domain sold to PetSmart.com</h4><p>$375,000 for the domain names and IP. The sock puppet is excluded.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Dec 28</b>2000</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Proxy: “up to approximately $0.25”</h4><p>The board asks stockholders to approve dissolution.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-24">24</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Jan 18</b>2001</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Dissolved and delisted</h4><p>Renamed IPET Holdings. Last close: $0.125. The remaining staff resign.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Sep 28</b>2001</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Payout #1: $0.09</h4><p>Initial liquidating distribution.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-8">8</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="marketing"><div class="d"><b>May 2002</b></div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Marketing</div><h4>Puppet joins 1-800-BarNone</h4><p>The rights were bought for $125,000. New slogan: “Everyone deserves a second chance.”<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-17">17</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-18">18</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Sep 27</b>2002</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Payout #2: $0.075</h4><p>Second liquidating distribution.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-9">9</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Jun 22</b>2004</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Final payout: $0.00747</h4><p>The shares are declared worthless.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-10">10</a></sup></p></div></div><div class="ev" data-cat="death"><div class="d"><b>Jun 3</b>2005</div><div class="dot"></div><div><div class="c">Death & estate</div><h4>Registration ends</h4><p>Form 15 filed. The company leaves the public record.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-23">23</a></sup></p></div></div></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="people" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">10</span>The people</div><h2 class="rv">Who ran it</h2><p class="lede rv">Ages and titles are from the IPO prospectus. Later careers are included only where we found a source.</p></div>
<div class="people"><div class="person rv"><div class="av">JW</div><h3>Julie Wainwright</h3><div class="role">Chairman &amp; CEO, Mar 1999 – 2001</div><p>Age 42 in the prospectus. She had been CEO of Reel.com and Berkeley Systems, and started in brand management at Clorox. She holds a BS from Purdue.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p><div class="then">Later: Founded the luxury consignment company The RealReal (2011).<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-37">37</a></sup></div></div><div class="person rv"><div class="av">CD</div><h3>Chris Deyo</h3><div class="role">President, from Apr 1999</div><p>Age 40. Previously president of Reel.com and earlier at Procter &amp; Gamble. Owned 1.8% after the IPO.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div><div class="person rv"><div class="av">PM</div><h3>Paul Manca</h3><div class="role">CFO, from Sep 1999</div><p>Age 41. Previously CFO of CellNet Data Systems and a banker at BZW/Barclays. He took the company through its IPO and the Merrill Lynch sale process.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div><div class="person rv"><div class="av">JH</div><h3>John Hommeyer</h3><div class="role">VP Marketing, from May 1999</div><p>Age 33. Previously Marketing Director for global baby care at Procter &amp; Gamble. He ran the marketing push that made the sock puppet famous.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div><div class="person rv"><div class="av">GM</div><h3>Greg McLemore</h3><div class="role">Founder, WebMagic</div><p>The archived 1999 About page says the site was “headed by Greg McLemore”. He held 5.1% after the IPO.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-36">36</a></sup></p></div><div class="person rv"><div class="av">JH</div><h3>John Hummer</h3><div class="role">Director; Hummer Winblad</div><p>His venture firm owned 21.2% before the IPO and 15.8% after, making it the second-largest holder after Amazon.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p></div><div class="person rv"><div class="av">MB</div><h3>Michael Ian Black</h3><div class="role">Voice of the sock puppet</div><p>The comedian performed the puppet in the TBWA&#92;Chiat&#92;Day campaign.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-13">13</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-16">16</a></sup></p><div class="then">Later: The puppet outlived the company and was relaunched by 1-800-BarNone in 2002.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-18">18</a></sup></div></div><div class="person rv"><div class="av">RC</div><h3>Richard G. Couch</h3><div class="role">Sole director, IPET Holdings</div><p>After dissolution he ran the wind-down from Danville, CA, overseeing three liquidating distributions totaling $0.172 a share.<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-6">6</a></sup><sup class="fn"><a href="#src-10">10</a></sup></p></div></div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="press">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">11</span>The clippings</div><h2 class="rv">What they said at the time</h2></div>
<div class="press"><div class="clip rv big"><q>This is a company’s dream… We’re happy because he’s captured their pocketbooks, too.</q><div class="who"><b>Julie Wainwright</b>, to Forbes, on the sock puppet, as quoted in Slate, Jun 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-14">14</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv"><q>It looks an awful lot like they’ve built that brand for a lousy business.</q><div class="who"><b>Slate</b>, “The Puppet That Ate a Dot-Com,” Jun 6, 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-14">14</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv"><q>Pets.com IPO is a dog</q><div class="who"><b>CNET News.com</b> headline on IPO day, Feb 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-12">12</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv"><q>Shipping huge bags of dog food doesn’t sound like a great profit opportunity.</q><div class="who"><b>Slate</b>, Jun 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-14">14</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv big"><q>Out of the more than 50 prospects contacted, fewer than 8 were even prepared to visit with the company.</q><div class="who"><b>Pets.com press release</b>, Nov 7, 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv"><q>I am deeply saddened by this event and regret that we will not be able to continue our commitment to our customers…</q><div class="who"><b>Julie Wainwright</b>, Nov 7, 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-5">5</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv"><q>The company spent a hideous amount of money to reach a lot of customers… who adore the ads but don’t have pets.</q><div class="who"><b>eCompany Now</b>, 2001<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-15">15</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv"><q>Down to zero.</q><div class="who"><b>Amazon.com spokeswoman</b>, on the value of its stake, LA Times, Nov 8, 2000<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-11">11</a></sup></div></div><div class="clip rv"><q>Dig another grave in the dot-com pet cemetery.</q><div class="who"><b>Los Angeles Times</b>, on Petopia, Feb 3, 2001<sup class="fn"><a href="#src-29">29</a></sup></div></div></div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="cause" class="alt">
<div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head"><div class="sec-k"><span class="sn">12</span>Cause of death</div><h2 class="rv">The autopsy</h2><p class="lede rv">Tap a cause to see the evidence. The percentages are our editorial weighting. The evidence under each one is sourced.</p></div>
<div class="cod" id="cod"></div>
<div class="disclaimer"><b>Editorial judgment:</b> the weights are an interpretive model by the Brand Obituary editors, not a measured quantity. The filings document the symptoms. How much each one contributed is a matter of opinion.</div>
</div>
</section>

<section id="fork">
<div class="wrap">
